{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Statistical Functions {#sec-chapter5}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Introduction\n",
        "In this chapter, we introduce statistical functions for probability distributions available in NumPy and SciPy. These libraries offer a wide range of tools for generating random numbers, evaluating probability density and cumulative distribution functions, and determining quantiles for various probability distributions. \n"
      ],
      "id": "6e1a2850"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import scipy.stats as stats"
      ],
      "id": "25c2c0f5",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "from IPython.core.interactiveshell import InteractiveShell\n",
        "InteractiveShell.ast_node_interactivity = \"all\""
      ],
      "id": "3b3d94e1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## NumPy functions\n",
        "The random number generators in NumPy are all stored in the submodule `np.random`. The key functions are listed below. \n",
        "\n",
        "* `rand/random_sample`:  Generates uniform random numbers from $[0, 1)$.\n",
        "* `randn/standard_normal`:  Generates random numbers from the standard normal distribution.\n",
        "* `randint/random_integers`: Generates random integer from the interval `[low,high)`.\n",
        "* `shuffle`:  Randomly reorders the elements of an array in place.\n",
        "* `permutation`: Returns randomly reordered elements of an array.\n",
        "* `binomial`: Draw samples from the binomial distribution.\n",
        "* `chisquare`: Generates draws from the chi-squared distribution.\n",
        "* `exponential`: Generates a draw from the exponential distribution.\n",
        "* `f(v_1,v_2)`: Generates draws from $F_{v_1,v_2}$ distribution.\n",
        "* `gamma`: Generates draws from gamma distribution.\n",
        "* `laplace`: Generates draws from the Laplace (Double Exponential) distribution.\n",
        "* `lognormal`: Generates draws from the log-normal distribution.\n",
        "* `multinomial`: Generates draws from the multinomial distribution.\n",
        "* `multivariate_normal`: Generates draws from the multivariate normal distribution.\n",
        "* `normal`:  Generates from the normal distribution.\n",
        "* `poisson`: Generates from the poisson distribution.\n",
        "* `standard_t`: Generates draws from the Student's $t$ distribution.\n",
        "* `uniform`: Generates draws from the interval $(0,1)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Some illustrative examples are provided below. "
      ],
      "id": "74ce237e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Generate 3x2x3 array of draws from the uniform distribution on [0,1)\n",
        "x = np.random.rand(3, 2, 3)\n",
        "x"
      ],
      "id": "3e11263b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Generate 3x2 array of draws from the standard normal distribution\n",
        "y = np.random.standard_normal((3, 2))\n",
        "y"
      ],
      "id": "15dc336d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Randomly order the elements of an array\n",
        "x = np.arange(10)\n",
        "np.random.shuffle(x)\n",
        "x"
      ],
      "id": "ff7dc1bf",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Generate 10 draws from N(mu, sigma)\n",
        "mu, sigma = 2, 1.5\n",
        "s = np.random.normal(mu, sigma, 10)\n",
        "s"
      ],
      "id": "0695ae0d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Generate 20 draws from Binomial(n, p)\n",
        "n, p = 10, 0.5  # number of trials, probability of each trial\n",
        "s = np.random.binomial(n, p, 20)\n",
        "s"
      ],
      "id": "e1b81dba",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Generate 4 draws from the chi-squared distribution with 2 degrees of freedom\n",
        "nu, n = 2, 4  # degrees of freedom, number of draws\n",
        "np.random.chisquare(nu, n)"
      ],
      "id": "2cd177f6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Generate 5 draws from the Student's t distribution with 10 degrees of freedom\n",
        "np.random.standard_t(df=10, size=5)"
      ],
      "id": "c90ddc40",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Computer-simulated random numbers are not truly random; they are generally referred to as pseudo-random numbers. The function `np.random.seed` is useful for initializing the random number generator, allowing for the generation of the same sequence of random numbers by setting the seed. In the example below, we set the seed to 12345 and generate 5 random draws from the standard normal distribution twice. Since we use the same seed in each case, we obtain the same random numbers in both instances."
      ],
      "id": "29112dd6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Set the seed for reproducibility\n",
        "np.random.seed(12345)\n",
        "x = np.random.randn(5)\n",
        "x\n",
        "np.random.seed(12345)\n",
        "y = np.random.randn(5)\n",
        "y"
      ],
      "id": "bbb70287",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## SciPy functions\n",
        "The probability distribution functions in SciPy are stored in the `scipy.stats` module. We import this module under the alias `stats`. We list some of important distributions from this module below: \n",
        "\n",
        "* `norm`: Normal distribution.\n",
        "* `beta`: Beta distribution.\n",
        "* `cauchy`: Cauchy distribution.\n",
        "* `chi2`: Chi-squared distribution.\n",
        "* `expon`: Exponential distribution.\n",
        "* `exponpow`: Exponential power distribution\n",
        "* `f`: F distribution.\n",
        "* `Gamma`: Gamma distribution.\n",
        "* `laplace`: Laplace, double exponential distribution.\n",
        "* `lognorm`: Lognormal distribution.\n",
        "* `t`: Student's t distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "These distribution functions assume the following methods: \n",
        "\n",
        "* `rvs`:  Generates pseudo-random numbers.\n",
        "* `pdf`: Returns probability density function.\n",
        "* `logpdf`: Returns log probability density function.\n",
        "* `cdf`: Returns cumulative distribution function.\n",
        "* `ppf`: Inverse CDF evaluation for an array of values between 0 and 1.\n",
        "* `fit`: Estimates shape, location, and scale parameters from data by maximum likelihood using an array of data.\n",
        "* `median/mean`: Returns median/mean of the distribution.\n",
        "* `var/std`: Returns variance/standard deviation of the distribution.\n",
        "* `moment`: Returns the $n$th non-central moment of the distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "The documentation on these methods is given at [Statistical functions](https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/stats.html). Some illustrative examples are shown below. "
      ],
      "id": "d002b4ec"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Generate 4 draws from N(2,9)\n",
        "stats.norm.rvs(loc=2, scale=3, size=4)"
      ],
      "id": "7387298c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Evaluate the pdf of the standard normal distribution at 1.96\n",
        "stats.norm.pdf(1.96, loc=0, scale=1)"
      ],
      "id": "e15450b2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Evaluate the cdf of the standard normal distribution at -1.96\n",
        "stats.norm.cdf(-1.96, loc=0, scale=1)"
      ],
      "id": "5de9a874",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Return the 0.95 quantile of N(0,1)\n",
        "stats.norm.ppf(0.95, loc=0, scale=1)"
      ],
      "id": "39218641",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Fit the normal distribution to data\n",
        "x = stats.norm.rvs(loc=1, scale=5, size=1000)\n",
        "location, scale = stats.norm.fit(x)\n",
        "print(\"(location, scale)=\", (location, scale))"
      ],
      "id": "984ce2f1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Return the median of N(3,1)\n",
        "stats.norm.median(loc=3, scale=1)"
      ],
      "id": "41bfe8b9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Return the mean of N(3,4)\n",
        "stats.norm.mean(loc=3, scale=2)"
      ],
      "id": "b784911e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Return the mean of chi-squared distribution with 4 degrees of freedom\n",
        "stats.chi2.mean(df=4)"
      ],
      "id": "1d514d52",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Return the variance of N(3,4)\n",
        "stats.norm.var(loc=3, scale=2)"
      ],
      "id": "7b08fcc3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Return the standard deviation of N(3,4)\n",
        "stats.norm.std(loc=3, scale=2)"
      ],
      "id": "2e033929",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Return the second non-central moment of N(0,1)\n",
        "stats.norm.moment(2, loc=0, scale=1)"
      ],
      "id": "1fe5c866",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    }
  ],
